== the java slice's codec bench ==
java.version=17.0.20  vm=17.0.20+8-1-24.04-Ubuntu
protobuf-java=protobuf-java-3.25.5.jar
lib=/home/user/ArmoniK.Api/ffi/poc/java/build/jni/libakjni.so
compact strings in the binding: yes (JDK 9+ String.value/coder)
rounds requested=36 (rounded up to a multiple of the arm count), per-round target 40 ms, arm order rotates every round
content set: ASCII (design/SHAPES.md's default; every id in the real schema is an ASCII GUID)
ak.deopt=0  (0 nothing; 1 one String.format with a numeric conversion, which is what R9 names;
           2 read a WIDE String through charAt; 3 read a LATIN1 String through charAt)

Every ratio is PAIRED: computed inside one round against that round's
own baseline, then reported as min/median/max across rounds. A ratio of
two medians from different rounds appears nowhere.


=== ENCODE ===
  pbj         toByteArray on a message serialised ONCE, which is what an application does: the size pass is paid, not memoized away
  pbj-reused-outa fresh message, written into a buffer the caller owns: the exact pair for `R` and `ffi`
  pbj-parsed  toByteArray on a message that was PARSED rather than built: its string fields hold ByteString, so it re-serialises with no UTF-8 transcoding at all
  pbj-loop    the same object serialised again and again, with the size memoized: what a loop benchmark reports, and not an application's cost
  pbj-reuse   writeTo a reused CodedOutputStream, size memoized: the fastest thing protobuf-java can be made to do here
  pbj-det     pbj-reuse with deterministic serialization, which is what sorts map entries and is what byte identity needs
  R           the generated pure-Java codec: README R3's no-boundary control, and section 13 outcome 2's architecture
  ffi         the C ABI, batched, the core transcoding
  ffi-nobatch the same, with the host declining to batch (ABI v1 section 6 allows it)
  ffi-zeroed  open decision 9's candidate: bulk-clear the chunk, assign only what differs
  R-take      arm R plus a fresh output array, so it and `pbj` deliver the same thing
  ffi-take    the C ABI, batched, plus a fresh output array, so it and `pbj` deliver the same thing

Comparable pairs. `pbj`, `R-take` and `ffi-take` all hand back a
fresh byte[] and all pay their own size pass, so those three are the
apples-to-apples set. `pbj-reuse`, `pbj-det`, `R`, `ffi`, `ffi-nobatch`
and `ffi-zeroed` all write into a buffer they already own -- but the
protobuf ones memoize the size and the others do not, so read those
against each other only through the deltas below.

Baseline: pbj. ns/op is that arm's own median. Each cell is min / median / max of
the PAIRED per-round ratio. Read the median; the min is the least
contaminated estimate and the max is usually one collection.

